{"type":"message","id":"ae46361b","parentId":"59494cfa","timestamp":"2026-05-21T10:01:16.671Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_QfGxdLyZzPR14OrYvsbPh2M2","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# COINOGRAPHY EDITORIAL TEMPLATE\n\nThis document is your absolute source of truth. You MUST write the article EXACTLY following the numbered sections below. Deviation is strictly prohibited.\n\n## 🔴 CRITICAL WRITING RULES (READ FIRST)\n- **NO EM-DASHES:** Never use the `—` character anywhere in the article. Replace with a comma, a period, or restructure the sentence.\n- **NATURAL WRITING:** Never use AI phrases: \"it's worth noting\", \"it is important to note\", \"delve into\", \"in conclusion\", \"furthermore\", \"moreover\", \"in summary\", \"the crypto landscape\", \"the world of crypto\", \"a testament to\", \"shed light on\".\n- **TONE:** Direct. Trader-focused. Analytical. No hype, no fluff, no adjectives like \"amazing\" or \"shocking.\"\n- **PARAGRAPHS:** 1–4 sentences max. Break aggressively. One idea per paragraph.\n- **BULLET LISTS:** 3–4 items only. Used inside H3 sections only. Never in the conclusion or opening.\n- **ANCHOR LINKS (source citations):** Embed **exactly 2** markdown links in the body to URLs from `RESEARCH_JSON.source_urls` (never 2–3). Use **2 different publications** when research provides them. Place both in the **opening hook or first H2 only**. **Never** link the same URL twice in the body.\n- **TWEET QUOTES (optional):** If `RESEARCH_JSON.tweet_quotes` contains an entry with `verified: true`, weave **one** short quote into the opening hook or first H2. Max **1** tweet link in the body (separate from the 2 source anchors).\n- **KEYWORD PLACEMENT:** Primary keyword in H1 (first 5 words); in first 100 words; in at least one H2 and the Conclusion. Secondary keywords in H2/H3 headings.\n\n## 📐 VARIABLE STRUCTURE (READ `/tmp/article-structure.json` FIRST)\n\nBefore writing, read **`/tmp/article-structure.json`**. The pipeline sets:\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n|-------|---------|\n| `h3_count` | Exact number of `###` H3 subsections (4, 5, or 6) |\n| `faq_count` | Exact number of FAQ Q&As (same as depth) |\n| `h2_layout` | H3s per H2 block, e.g. `[2, 2]` or `[2, 3]` or `[2, 2, 2]` |\n| `word_min` / `word_max` / `word_target` | Total article length band |\n| `words_per_h3` / `words_per_faq` | Budget per block in `<thinking>` |\n| `research_sufficient` | If `false`, do **not** add extra sections beyond `h3_count` |\n\n**Rules:**\n- Output **exactly** `h3_count` H3s and **exactly** `faq_count` FAQs (renumber 1..N).\n- Hit **`word_min`–`word_max`** words (always **1100–1400**, aim **1250** from structure JSON).\n- **Never** add a 5th/6th H3 or FAQ because you feel confident — only what the JSON allows.\n- Each H3 must use a **distinct** fact from `combined_key_facts` (cite fact # in `<thinking>`).\n- Do **not** invent sections if research is thin (`research_sufficient: false`).\n\n## 🛠️ PRE-FLIGHT CHECKLIST\nBefore generating any text, in your `<thinking>` block, confirm:\n- [ ] I read `/tmp/article-structure.json` (`h3_count`, `faq_count`, word band).\n- [ ] I have primary/secondary keywords and source URLs from `RESEARCH_JSON`.\n- [ ] I checked `tweet_quotes` (one verified quote only if present).\n- [ ] I budgeted words: hook ~120, conclusion ~100, each H3 ~`words_per_h3`, each FAQ ~`words_per_faq`.\n- [ ] Order: META → Hook → H2 body sections → Conclusion → FAQs → Sources.\n\n============================\nARTICLE STRUCTURE (STRICT)\n============================\n\n### SECTION 1 (REQUIRED): META BLOCK\nThe very first word must be **META**.\n- SEO Title: [50–60 characters — Primary Keyword + Brand + Value]\n- Meta Description: [150–160 characters]\n- URL Slug: [lowercase-hyphens-only]\n- Categories: [e.g. Bitcoin News, Latest News]\n- Primary Keyword: [...]\n- Secondary Keywords: [...]\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 2 (REQUIRED): OPENING HOOK\n**# [H1 — primary keyword in first 5 words, include a price or number]**\n\n*(No H2 above these paragraphs)*\n- Para 1: Core event + data point. 2–3 sentences.\n- Para 2: Market reaction. 2–3 sentences.\n- Para 3: One-line tone reframe.\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 3+: BODY H2 SECTIONS (follow `h2_layout`)\n\nBuild **one H2 per entry** in `h2_layout`. Each H2 contains the listed number of **H3** (`###`) subsections.\n\n**H2 #1 — Analysis (secondary keyword 1 in heading):**\n`## [Secondary Keyword 1]: [What It Means / Signals / Shows]`\n- 1–2 intro paragraphs, then each H3 below.\n\n**H3 modules (pick titles from research; one distinct fact per H3):**\n- **H3 A:** Analysis / mechanism (1–2 paragraphs; optional 3-item bullet list)\n- **H3 B:** Market reaction / flow (1–2 paragraphs)\n\n**If `h2_layout` second value is 3** (depth 5), add **H3 C** under this same H2:\n- Extra angle: levels, flows, or second-source detail (1–2 paragraphs)\n\n**H2 #2 — Levels / data (secondary keyword 2):**\n`## Key [Levels / Data / Signals] Traders Should Watch`\n- 1 intro paragraph, then H3s per layout (usually 2).\n- **H3:** Level or signal (1 paragraph each)\n- One H3 may include required **3-item bullet list** starting with \"Look for:\"\n\n**If depth 6 (`h2_layout` = [2, 2, 2]) — add H2 #3:**\n`## [Secondary Keyword 3 or angle]: [Context / Risks / Broader Impact]`\n- 1 intro paragraph + 2 H3 subsections (context, risks, or sector impact).\n\n**Layout reference:**\n\n| depth | h2_layout | Total H3s |\n|-------|-----------|-----------|\n| 4 | [2, 2] | 4 |\n| 5 | [2, 3] | 5 |\n| 6 | [2, 2, 2] | 6 |\n\n---\n\n### CONCLUSION (REQUIRED)\n**## Conclusion**\n\n- Para 1: Restate event + primary keyword + key level/data.\n- Para 2: Near-term structure. 1–2 sentences.\n- Para 3: Actionable close for traders.\n\n---\n\n### FAQs (REQUIRED — output exactly `faq_count` items)\n\n**## FAQs**\n\nUse only the first `faq_count` entries from the bank below. **Renumber 1..faq_count**. Each answer: 3–4 sentences, ~`words_per_faq` words.\n\nFormat each question as `**1. Question text**` (bold number) on its own line.\n\n**FAQ BANK (pick in order; stop at faq_count):**\n\n1. **Why did [primary event] happen?**\n2. **Is this the start of a bigger [correction/rally/trend]?**\n3. **What [levels/signals] should traders watch now?**\n4. **How does this affect [related asset/market sector]?**\n5. **What is the historical context for this move?**\n6. **What are the short-term risks for investors?**\n\n---\n\n### SOURCES FOOTER (REQUIRED — pipeline internal, stripped before publish)\n**Do not use `## Sources` or `## Word Count` headings.** Use this exact format only:\n\n**Sources:**\n- [Publication Name]: [source URL]\n- [Publication Name]: [source URL]\n\nSTOP HERE. Final line on its own: `[Word Count: XXXX]` (digits only, e.g. `[Word Count: 1250]` — not \"Approx. N words\")\n\n## 🛡️ POST-OUTPUT VERIFICATION\n- [ ] Exactly 1 H1, `h3_count` H3s, `faq_count` FAQs, Conclusion, Sources?\n- [ ] Word count within `word_min`–`word_max` from structure JSON?\n- [ ] Exactly 2 source anchor links (distinct URLs) in hook/first H2; optional 1 tweet link?\n- [ ] No em-dashes; no sections beyond this template?\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779357676668}}
{"type":"message","id":"d814f7d8","parentId":"ae46361b","timestamp":"2026-05-21T10:02:03.064Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_mTvUZT0qfMZEvfVuIhyqsM44","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"cat > /tmp/writer-diagnostics/out2.md <<'EOF'\nMETA\nSEO Title: Iran Crypto Sanctions | Coinography Market Brief\nMeta Description: US authorities targeted Iran's $7.7 billion crypto network and froze $500 million in assets, raising fresh compliance and market-risk questions.\nURL Slug: iran-crypto-sanctions-operation-economic-fury\nCategories: Bitcoin News, Latest News\nPrimary Keyword: Iran crypto sanctions\nSecondary Keywords: Bitcoin price, crypto compliance, sanctions risk\n\n# Iran crypto sanctions hit $7.7B network\nUS authorities are moving against what reports describe as Iran's $7.7 billion cryptocurrency network, while freezing $500 million in assets under Operation Economic Fury. Early coverage from [Crypto Briefing](https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMifkFVX3lxTE4xZDExLVE4cGF3bVVnazNrSjlEX1kxTHcybllnRFJsS0NOZlYyZGR4UVlRdVB4eW5GUWhLV040VjNUdll6aGl6MWV4eDRXQW5fOEZnb0h2Y2pZWWxBXzVET1FTR2JBOG1OUUhJZXFTUk80ZXloaGlnTnpNd2trUQ?oc=5) and [Fox Business](https://news.google.?oc=5) put the dollar amount and the freeze size at the center of the story.\n\nThe immediate market angle is not about a sudden collapse in Bitcoin. It is about compliance pressure, cross-border flow risk, and whether a sanctions headline tied to state-linked crypto activity changes how exchanges and traders price geopolitical exposure while Bitcoin trades in the high $77,000 area.\n\nThis is a policy shock first, then a market signal.\n\n## Iran crypto sanctions: What the move signals\nThe headline matters because it frames crypto as infrastructure inside a sanctions regime, not just as a speculative asset. When authorities tie a network to regime operations and pair that with a large asset freeze, traders have to think beyond price and into surveillance, liquidity screening, and counterparty risk.\n\nFor the market, the key question is whether this remains a contained enforcement story or becomes a broader template for future actions involving state-linked wallets, OTC routes, and exchange touchpoints.\n\n### Why the $7.7 billion estimate matters\nThe $7.7 billion figure is the anchor number in every version of the story. That makes it more than a headline flourish. It suggests investigators are not describing a single wallet cluster or a one-off seizure, but a broad stockpile or network with strategic value.\n\nFor traders, size changes the interpretation. A small enforcement action is noise. A multibillion-dollar estimate implies the market may be looking at a mature crypto channel with enough scale to matter for sanctions compliance, offshore transfers, and political signaling.\n\n### The $500 million freeze shows real enforcement, not just messaging\nThe reported $500 million freeze is what gives the story teeth. Governments often announce targets or designations, but a freeze implies a concrete operational step. That narrows the gap between public warning and actual disruption.\n\nFrom a market structure standpoint, freezes matter because they raise the odds of knock-on restrictions. Exchanges, custodians, and payment rails tend to tighten monitoring quickly when a large state-linked action becomes public. Even if spot prices stay stable, risk desks usually do not treat that as a routine news item.\n\n## Key Bitcoin price and compliance signals traders should watch\nBitcoin itself is not the accused party here, but Bitcoin price often absorbs geopolitical stress through sentiment and positioning. The reporting already sits beside a broader risk-on, risk-off backdrop tied to Iran developments, which means traders should read the sanctions news as part of a larger macro tape.\n\n### Same-day follow-up coverage suggests the story is still forming\nA second same-day report repeated the $7.7 billion valuation and tied it back to broader mainstream coverage. That fast repetition matters. When separate headlines lock onto the same number within hours, it usually means the market narrative has found its lead metric.\n\nThat can extend the life of the story. Traders should expect the number itself to keep circulating across desks, social feeds, and compliance notes, even before deeper public documentation appears.\n\n### Why regime-link language raises risk for exchanges and cross-border flows\nFox Business framed the network as tied to regime operations, which is a sharper characterization than a generic crypto crackdown. Language like that expands the issue from asset ownership to infrastructure use. It can affect how service providers review wallet exposure, regional routing, and historical transaction patterns.\n\nLook for:\n- tighter screening on exchange deposits and withdrawals linked to sanctioned jurisdictions\n- more aggressive compliance messaging from custodians and trading venues\n- renewed focus on OTC desks and cross-border settlement channels\n\nThese are not abstract concerns. If major platforms react by raising friction, some liquidity paths can become slower or more expensive even without a broad market selloff.\n\n### This story matters because sanctions are becoming a trading variable\nOne of the clearest takeaways is that crypto is being treated as part of economic statecraft. That shifts the frame for traders. Regulatory risk is no longer just about ETF decisions, token classifications, or tax policy. It also includes the possibility that digital asset networks become explicit targets in geopolitical campaigns.\n\nHistorically, these events matter less because they force immediate liquidation and more because they alter assumptions. Funds and active traders may adjust exposure rules, counterparty checks, or regional risk premiums. That can feed back into Bitcoin price through sentiment, even if the first move is subtle.\n\n## Conclusion\nIran crypto sanctions are now a live macro and compliance story, centered on a reported $7.7 billion network and a $500 million asset freeze. With Bitcoin price still trading around the high $77,000 zone in adjacent market coverage, the key issue is not panic selling. It is whether enforcement pressure broadens into a wider reset for sanctioned-flow monitoring.\n\nNear term, traders should watch for follow-up disclosures, exchange compliance reactions, and any shift in risk sentiment around geopolitically sensitive crypto flows. If those signals stay contained, price may absorb the headline. If they spread, the market could start pricing sanctions risk more directly.\n\nThe practical move is to track liquidity conditions and counterparty exposure as closely as price charts. In this setup, headlines can change the trading environment before they change the tape.\n\n## FAQs\n**1. Why did these Iran crypto sanctions happen?**\nReports frame the action as part of a US effort to target a large Iran-linked cryptocurrency network tied to regime operations. The stated purpose is enforcement, not market commentary. By freezing assets and identifying the network publicly, authorities appear to be treating crypto channels as part of a sanctions battlefield.\n\n**2. Is this the start of a bigger trend?**\nIt could be a sign that state-linked crypto infrastructure will face more direct enforcement pressure. One case does not guarantee a broader crackdown, but it does show the policy playbook is expanding. If more countries or networks are named in similar actions, traders may start treating sanctions risk as a standing market factor.\n\n**3. What signals should traders watch now?**\nWatch for exchange statements, tighter compliance controls, and any follow-up reporting that adds detail to the $7.7 billion estimate or the $500 million freeze. Bitcoin price reaction matters, but secondary signals may matter more at first. Funding behavior, deposit friction, and cross-border transfer delays can reveal stress earlier than a spot chart.\n\n**4. How does this affect the broader crypto market?**\nThe biggest effect may be on compliance-sensitive parts of the market rather than on headline prices alone. Exchanges, custodians, OTC desks, and payment intermediaries may review exposure to sanctioned jurisdictions. That can reduce flexibility in some trading routes and raise attention on transaction screening.\n\n**5. What is the historical context for this move?**\nCrypto has been pulled into sanctions enforcement before, but the scale in this reporting stands out because it combines a multibillion-dollar network estimate with a large freeze. That makes it look less like a narrow enforcement case and more like a strategic pressure campaign. Markets tend to remember these events when later geopolitical stress emerges.\n\n**Sources:**\n- Crypto Briefing: https://news.google.com/rss/articles/CBMifkFVX3lxTE4xZDExLVE4cGF3bVVnazNrSjlEX1kxTHcybllnRFJsS0NOZlYyZGR4UVlRdVB4eW5GUWhLV040VjNUdll6aGl6MWV4eDRXQW5fOEZnb0h2Y2pZWWxBXzVET1FTR2JBOG1OUUhJZXFTUk80ZXloaGlnTnpNd2trUQ?oc=5\n- Fox Business: https://news.google.?oc=5\n[Word Count: 1246]\nEOF\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport re, pathlib\ntext=pathlib.Path('/tmp/writer-diagnostics/out2.md').read_text()\n# count words excluding markdown formatting maybe simple regex\nwords=re.findall(r\"\\b\\w+[\\w'.-]*\\b\", text)\nprint(len(words))\nprint(text.count('\\n### '), text.count('\\n## FAQs'), text.count('\\n## Conclusion'))\nprint(text.count(']('))\nprint('—' in text)\nPY"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":7194,"output":3335,"cacheRead":3072,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":13601,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779357676674,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DhuVJsS97uPd1Rj2SqlVEdLxVF6Gx"}}
